WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.719
What is a spoon made of? What is
water made of? What are you made of?

2
00:00:08.939 --> 00:00:12.933
The same answer every time:
particles, far too small to see.

3
00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:18.541
Everything is made of particles: bits
far too small for any eye to see.

4
00:00:18.761 --> 00:00:23.823
A spoon, water, the air, you. All
of them are made of particles.

5
00:00:24.043 --> 00:00:29.709
So scientists use a model: a picture
of an idea, drawn so you can see it.

6
00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:34.851
This is the particle model. Forty balls
in a box, standing for the real thing.

7
00:00:35.351 --> 00:00:40.413
In a solid the particles are packed tightly
in rows. Every one touches the next.

8
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:45.974
They cannot move about. But they
vibrate: they jiggle on the spot.

9
00:00:46.194 --> 00:00:51.209
Because they hold their rows, a solid
keeps its shape. A brick stays a brick.

10
00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:55.655
Do the particles in a block of
ice stop moving? No, they do not.

11
00:00:55.875 --> 00:00:59.405
They vibrate all the time.
Only a little, but always.

12
00:00:59.905 --> 00:01:05.338
Now heat the solid. Heat gives the
particles energy, so they vibrate harder.

13
00:01:05.558 --> 00:01:10.202
Heat it more and they break out of
their rows. The solid has melted.

14
00:01:10.422 --> 00:01:14.695
In a liquid the particles still
touch, but they slide past each other.

15
00:01:14.915 --> 00:01:19.048
That is why a liquid flows and
takes the shape of its container.

16
00:01:19.268 --> 00:01:25.352
Cool it and they slow down and lock back into
rows. The same particles all the way through.

17
00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:30.960
Scientists keep three words apart:
material, substance and particle.

18
00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:37.310
A material is what a thing is made of. Wood
is a material, and a mixture of substances.

19
00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:42.592
A substance is one pure kind of stuff, the
same all the way through. Water is one.

20
00:01:42.812 --> 00:01:46.620
A particle is one of the tiny
bits a substance is made of.

21
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.253
Pour sand into a cup and it takes
the cup's shape, like a liquid.

22
00:01:51.473 --> 00:01:54.353
But sand is a solid. Look
at one grain through a lens.

23
00:01:54.573 --> 00:01:59.681
The grain keeps its shape. Inside
it the particles are packed in rows.

24
00:01:59.901 --> 00:02:05.102
So what slides is not the particles. It
is the grains, rolling over each other.

25
00:02:05.322 --> 00:02:09.316
Salt, sugar and flour are
powders too: solids that pour.

26
00:02:09.816 --> 00:02:13.624
The particle model is useful,
and it is not a photograph.

27
00:02:13.844 --> 00:02:18.163
Real particles have no colour. The
green is for us, so we can see them.

28
00:02:18.383 --> 00:02:23.120
They are far smaller than the picture,
and they come in many kinds and shapes.

29
00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:28.309
The model shows forty. One drop of
water has more than you could count.

30
00:02:28.809 --> 00:02:34.196
In a solid the particles sit in rows
and vibrate. In a liquid they slide.

31
00:02:34.416 --> 00:02:37.806
They never stop moving, and
heat makes them move more.

32
00:02:38.026 --> 00:02:41.648
A powder pours because its
grains roll, not its particles.

33
00:02:41.868 --> 00:02:45.166
And the model is a picture
of an idea, not a photograph.
